Bug 2060192 - Keep a folder's color when the folder is renamed. r=mkmelin,aleca

Folder colors are stored in `folderTree.json`, keyed by the folder URI.
Renaming a folder changes its URI and the URI of every folder below it, so
the stored colors stayed behind under the old keys and the folders fell back
to their default color.

`FolderTreeProperties` owns that store, so it now follows its own keys. A
`folderRenamed` listener re-keys the folder and its subfolders. Doing this in
the callers instead would mean three places, and none of them should have to
know that folder colors exist.

After re-keying it notifies `folder-color-changed` without a color, which
makes the tree rows read the store again. That is needed because for local
folders the rows are already built when the notification arrives, while for
IMAP they are built afterwards.

Moving a folder inside a local or EWS account also changes the URI and is not
fixed here. `folderMoveCopyCompleted` only names the destination parent, and
looking the folder up by name there hits the wrong folder when the
destination already holds that name, because such a move gets renamed to
"name(2)". Moving an IMAP folder is covered, because IMAP moves by renaming.

Differential Revision: https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D316204


